save(i,dict(title='Does noisy parity learning require Ω(n²/ε²) memory or exponentially many samples?',formal='Do there exist absolute constants c₁,c₂>0 and n₀ such that, for every integer n≥n₀, every 0<ε<1/2 and every one-pass learner recovering a uniformly random x∈{0,1}^n with probability at least 2/3 from the noisy parity samples defined below, its worst-case memory S and maximum sample count T satisfy S≥c₁n²/ε² or T≥2^{c₂n}? Constants are independent of n, ε and the learner, and ε may tend to zero with n. This states the bounded-error recovery interpretation of Conjecture 1; it does not additionally require a lower bound at exponentially small success probability.',definitions='Arithmetic in 𝔽₂={0,1} uses addition and multiplication modulo two; ⟨a,x⟩=Σᵢ₌₁ⁿaᵢxᵢ mod 2. First choose x uniformly from {0,1}^n and hold it fixed. Independently for each t, choose a_t uniformly from {0,1}^n and a noise bit Z_t with Pr[Z_t=1]=1/2−ε; observe (a_t,b_t) with b_t=⟨a_t,x⟩+Z_t mod 2. Noise bits, query vectors and internal learner coins are mutually independent. The learner outputs one estimate x̂; success means x̂=x, and its probability averages over x, all observations and internal coins. The learner sees each sample once in order, cannot choose a_t or revisit discarded observations, and has no computational-time restriction. Formally use a branching program with T+1 layers and width at most 2^S, one start vertex and one outgoing edge for each possible sample at each nonterminal vertex, all going to the next layer. Leaves, possibly in earlier layers, are labelled by estimates in {0,1}^n. Randomized learners may use fresh coins to choose state updates; retained coins are part of the state. Equivalently, the lower bound may be checked on deterministic nonuniform programs, since a random tape can be fixed to achieve at least the average success under this fixed input distribution without increasing width or length. The program may depend on n and ε; its transition description, current layer and input sample are not charged as stored data. Memory counts persistent state bits, S=⌈log₂(width)⌉, and T is the maximum number of consumed samples, not its expectation. No hypothesis is made about the hardness of LPN in unrestricted computation.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove the universal bounded-error memory/sample tradeoff with constants uniform in ε, or refute that asymptotic claim by showing that no such constants can work. A bound Ω(n²/ε), a result only for constant ε, or a lower bound in a restricted statistical-query model does not settle the proposed dependence on noise.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Noise reduces the information in each equation',text='Without noise, every observation is a correct linear relation about the hidden vector. Here the relation is only slightly more likely to be right than wrong when ε is small. The question asks whether this forces a quadratic loss in the storage threshold as the bias tends to zero.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A specific gap between known memory bounds',text='The source proves that a learner needs Ω(n²/ε) memory or exponentially many samples, and records an upper bound using O(n²/ε²) memory and O(n/ε²) samples. The conjecture asks whether the stronger memory dependence is necessary below an exponential sample budget.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Unrestricted work between observations',text='The lower bound concerns retained information, not the time needed to process it. It must hold for arbitrary state-transition rules and therefore would be unconditional even though efficient learning of noisy parity is also used as a computational hardness assumption.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The bias must remain a parameter',text='For ε fixed away from zero, the two memory bounds differ only by constants. The unresolved content is their uniform dependence on ε, together with an exponential sample threshold whose exponent does not shrink with ε.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Subsequent work still treats the tradeoff as a goal',text='The 2024 DIMACS project abstract identifies the same n²/ε²-versus-exponential tradeoff as its long-term goal and describes intermediate observations in a modified model. It is evidence of continued study, not a proved lower bound or a fresh claim that all later work has been exhausted.',citation='followup2024')],why='Would quantify how observation noise increases the storage needed for a central learning problem, strengthening unconditional limits relevant to streaming learning and bounded-storage cryptography.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2021',text='The published lower bound has memory dependence Ω(n²/ε); Conjecture 1 proposes Ω(n²/ε²) under a subexponential sample budget.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2024',text='A subsequent research-project abstract reports partial investigations and keeps the full tradeoff as its goal.',citation='followup2024')],status='source_open',status_note='Checked the complete conference statement and branching-program model, the arXiv version record, a 2024 follow-up abstract and the author’s 2026 publication list. No resolution was found through 12 September 2026. The listed 2026 planted-structure work has a different stated scope; its abstract, not its full proofs, was inspected.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Tests the precise storage cost of noise in a canonical learning task, separating information retention from unrestricted computation time. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Recovered the full conjectured n²/ε² threshold, noise convention, uniform hidden vector, one-pass branching-program model and maximum costs. Made explicit the conventional 2/3 bounded-error recovery interpretation; did not silently demand the stronger exponentially small-success theorem suggested by some lower-bound formulations.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['A hidden binary vector is observed through uniformly random parity equations with independent noise.','Each equation is correct with probability one half plus ε.','The proposed lower bound requires either Ω(n²/ε²) stored bits or exponentially many samples.','The source proves the weaker Ω(n²/ε) memory threshold and gives a matching-to-the-conjecture space upper bound.','The card fixes bounded-error exact recovery and requires constants uniform in ε.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','followup2024'])),notes=['Restored the complete conjectured tradeoff and uniform dependence on the noise bias.','Defined exact recovery, its probability space and the one-pass nonuniform branching-program model.','Made the bounded-error reading explicit and distinguished memory from computation time.','Checked later project and author records without presenting intermediate work as a solution.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00770'])
